T* (Joints are classified structurally and functionally.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Structural classes) Tj
T* (- Fibrous joints: bones joined by dense connective tissue; little or no movement. Examples:) Tj
T* (sutures of the skull, the gomphosis of a tooth in its socket.) Tj
T* (- Cartilaginous joints: bones joined by cartilage. Synchondroses \(for example, the) Tj
T* (epiphyseal plate\) and symphyses \(for example, the pubic symphysis, intervertebral discs\).) Tj
T* (- Synovial joints: freely movable; contain a joint capsule, synovial fluid and articular) Tj
T* (cartilage. Examples: knee, shoulder, hip.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Functional classes) Tj
T* (- Synarthrosis: immovable. Amphiarthrosis: slightly movable. Diarthrosis: freely movable) Tj
T* (\(all synovial joints\).) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Synovial joint types by movement) Tj
T* (- Hinge \(elbow\), pivot \(atlantoaxial joint\), ball-and-socket \(hip, shoulder\), saddle \(thumb) Tj
T* (carpometacarpal\), condyloid \(wrist\), plane \(intercarpal\).) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Clinical link: the rotator cuff stabilises the shoulder, a ball-and-socket joint with a) Tj
T* (shallow socket, which makes it highly mobile but prone to dislocation.) Tj
